From 41732bc65a717c32bcdf75b566e633e003c5a74b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 08:54:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] =?UTF-8?q?robo(reports):=20trust=20each=20slab's=20ow?=
 =?UTF-8?q?n=20stored=20date=20in=20the=20hourly=20chart=20=E2=80=94=20end?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20the=20date/hour=20drift?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The "Production Rate per Hour" chart placed slabs by reconstructing the day from
serialNumber order plus time-wrap detection. serialNumber is unreliable
(mistyped or duplicated on old runs), so the walk ran out of order, every
out-of-order step looked like a midnight crossing and advanced the day cursor,
and whole batches drifted forward by 1 to 15 days; the length cap then showed a
window on the wrong dates and dropped the real ones. That is why 1440 (8-10 Sep)
read as 23-25 Sep, 1445 (15-16 Sep) as 18-20 Sep, and a 19 Jul slab jumped to
20 Jul.

Place each slab on its OWN stored production date instead — the date the
operator actually entered (productionDateOf: the slab's own date, else the
setup's, else the shift's). In = date + In time; Out = date + Out time, rolled
to the next day only when Out precedes In (the overnight rule: In 23:55, Out
00:09 belongs to the next day). Count each slab in its Out hour; the timeline is
the earliest In to the latest Out, one bucket per hour with the calendar date
its hour belongs to. No serial number, no wall clock — the same records always
draw the same chart. A genuinely wrong stored date is corrected on the slab
(Slab Records -> Edit), not reconstructed away here, so the chart is a faithful
view of the records and every slab sits in exactly one correct date+hour bucket.

- hourlyProduction: rewritten to trust the stored date; HourlySlab reduced to
  {productionDate, inTime, outTime}; MAX_HOURS raised to 96 (four days) and the
  timeline anchored on the batch start so a lone forward-mis-dated row cannot
  drag it across empty days.
- reports/hourly route: stop selecting and ordering by serialNumber/createdAt;
  order no longer affects the result.
- tests: rewritten for trust-dates; add the audit's named batches (1384/1385,
  1386, 1440, 1445) plus the overnight and stray-date guards.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
